2026-07-20 22:41:52,248 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48458 (CSTEST-668): summary='Low-Level User can Toggle Activation Status in Admin Panel' description='h2. Threat Scenario\n\nA threat actor with access as an authenticated RI.Insights user can access an API endpoint which can change visual display state of the "status" indicator in the Users section of the RI.Insights portal.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nA threat actor with knowledge of this API endpoint could toggle the visual "status" display of any user in the system. This could cause users with access to the Users section of the RI.Insights portal to believe that a user has been falsely activated or deactivated in the system. Note that this action does not disable user access to the system, but only changes the visual indicator. Also note that if an Admin "re-enables" the user via the slider, this will trigger a password reset for the affected user.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nA threat actor could infer the request structure by either capturing it via an on-path (e.g. MITM) attack or constructing the request with reasonable understanding of the website functionality.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\n[preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/users-service/user/useraction|http://preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/users-service/user/useraction]\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nImplement proper access control on all administrator/high-level functions. Verify the status of each user in the back end of the website as well.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nWhen logged into the RI.Insights application as a user with Administrator permissions, the assessment team reviewed API calls made by the browser to the various RI.Insights API endpoints. The assessment team observed an API call to the /api/v1/users-service/user/useraction API endpoint when enabling or disabling a user account in the system, which included the action (enabled: true or enabled: false), and the User ID of the targeted user. The assessment team then authenticated to the system as a non-privileged user (surgeon and care team member), and using the Authorization Bearer token, re-issued the call to the useraction API. Note that this action could be performed against any user, assuming their User ID were known. The assessment team observed that, in the Users UI when logged in as an Administrator, the status of the user would change to the state issued by the non-privileged user\'s API call. This action did not enable or disable the targeted user, however it did affect the UI of the administrator, which could prove confusing to the admin. Further, the assessment team observed that if the Administrative user re-enabled a user after being set to disabled, this would trigger a password reset for the targeted user.' issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,249 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48459 (CSTEST-667): summary='Application Log Forging' description='h2. Threat Scenario\n\nAny authenticated user can forge logs generated from the web client to the logger service. A threat actor can abuse this ability to falsify application logs, submit excessive invalid logs, and undermine nonrepudiation of logged activity.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nA threat actor with knowledge of this vulnerability could falsify log data as it is sent from the client to the logger service. A threat actor could also leverage this vulnerability to flood logs with superfluous data.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nA threat actor with an authenticated session to the application can note issued requests to "logger-service" and observe that the requests contain a "UserId" field which may be modified.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\n[preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/logger-service/logger|http://preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/logger-service/logger]\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nLevel Nine recommends that Smith and Nephew validate inputs submitted to the website logger; associated user IDs should be validated or set by identifying the user associated with the authenticated session being utilized for the request. Additionally, where possible logging should be implemented server-side to minimize client/user control of log contents.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team observed requests to "logger-service" containing a "UserId" field. The team issued additional requests to the logger-service containing forged/modified fields. Subsequent review of activity logs in the application showed that the user ID was successfully forged.' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,251 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48460 (CSTEST-666): summary='Incomplete Deactivation of Users' description="h2. Threat Scenario\n\nWhen an account is deactivated any preexisting authenticated session for that account remains functional. A authenticated session will stay active until it times out or the user logs out. If an account is deactivated in response to a threat actor gaining access, the threat actor could continue to access account functionality with a preexisting authenticated session.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nThe threat actor with a preexisting authenticated session to an account can continue to abuse data and functionality in the account even after the account has been deactivated.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nA threat actor does not need knowledge of this weakness to abuse it. If the threat actor has a preexisting authenticated session to the application, they may continue to utilize the session after account deactivation.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\nRi.INSIGHTS\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nLevel Nine recommends Smith and Nephew invalidate a user's current session(s) immediately upon account deactivation.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team logged into the application as an Admin user in one browser and any other user in another browser. The user account was deactivated by the Admin account. Subsequently, the user could continue to use the preexisting authenticated session to interact with the application." issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,251 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48461 (CSTEST-665): summary='Denial of Service through Resource Consumption' description='h2. Threat Scenario\n\nThe assessment team observed that, if many devices are created in a short period of time, the RI.Insights application becomes unresponsive. A threat-actor with admin privileges can flood the application with device creation operations to consume excessive resources and affect the availability of the application.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nA threat actor can flood the application with device creation requests to cause excessive consumption of application resources and result in a Denial of Service (DoS) attack.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nA threat actor which has gained Admin privileges can observe the ability to create new devices in the Admin panel. The threat actor may infer that creating a device results in a resource consumption, that if repeatedly executed, could affect application availability.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\n[preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/users-service/deviceinfo|http://preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/users-service/deviceinfo]\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nLevel Nine recommends that Smith & Nephew enforce a rate limit for all functionality which may result in adverse resource consumption. Do this for all users including high privilege users such as administrators.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team issued many device creation requests. Each request incremented the "serialno" value in the request body such that each request created a new device. Initially, the requests completed successfully returning an HTTP 200 status code. Though, after approximately 1 minute, the assessment team also noticed that the requests started returning 500 "Internal Server Error" and 502 "Bad Gateway" responses which indicate application availability was impacted.' issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
