2026-07-20 22:41:52,507 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48520 (CSTEST-471): summary='ActiveArm: Manual Pose Control' description='The user should be able to quickly release the joint brakes and move the arm into a desired positioned.\n\nCommonly used positions may include:\n* Extended away from the operative table for placing the sterile drape https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-418\n* Above the patients chest in order to be out of the way during registration\n* Close to the surgical site for bone removal' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,507 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48521 (CSTEST-468): summary='ActiveArm: Active Compensation' description='The Active Compensation feature needs to automatically adjust the position of the robotic arm when the patients anatomy that it is operating on moves.' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,508 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48278 (CSTEST-467): summary='Capture Manual Femur Reference Definition - TKA' description='Currently, CORI allows users to choose between the PCA defined by the system or a manually defined rotational reference.\n\nProblem: The system does not store/capture what rotational reference was used and what the deviation from PCA was (in degrees) if the user chose to redefine their reference.\n\nThis has cascading effects on the data that is collected and interpreted through RI.INSIGHTS and the AI/COE data team. Any end users of the data are unable to quantify rotation of the femoral implant with respect to a standard/uniform reference.\n\nRequest a software change to CORI that allows the system to capture, store, and report in a case export, the original location of CORI-defined PCA and the degrees of deviation from that PCA if the reference was manually defined.' issueType='Epic' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,508 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48279 (CSTEST-466): summary='Release Refurbished Drill Tracker CI ROB10014S' description='When drill trackers are returned to complaints they need to be able to be refurbished and orderable as a service CI.  A CI needs to be created in Matrix and SAP.  Inspection criteria and processes need to be established.  Appropriate labeling updates need to be completed.\n\n+*Rationale for Importance*+\nThis will relieve inventory burden created from [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-454|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-454|smart-link]  resulting in immediate increase in CORI utilization' issueType='Epic' status='Review' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,509 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48522 (CSTEST-465): summary='ActiveArm: TKA Saw Cut Order Preference' description='Prior to beginning a procedure using the [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-457|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-457|smart-link]  the user should be able to define the desired cut order.  When the user enters bone removal each cut will appear in the order it was defined prior to beginning the case.\n\nThe cut order for a user should be able to be saved between cases so it does not need to be entered every time.  The user should also have the ability to edit their saved preference prior to a case.' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,509 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48523 (CSTEST-464): summary='Remedy Log4J security vulnerability' description='a Log4j security vulnerablity was release and we need to review and upgrade log4J in the Cumulus application.   Meg is working with security on doing an assessment across our product portfolio of the Log4J critical cybersecurity vulnerability. It’s an easily misused vulnerability for any externally-facing systems that are using versions of Apache before 2.15. Here is some more detailed information if you’re interested: https://unit42.paloaltonetworks.com/apache-log4j-vulnerability-cve-2021-44228/\n\nThe immediate risk for Cumulus is protected via the AWS WAF that has been implemented.   We have received the WAF configuration with Viktor(Lumino) and we do see the WAF rule blocking requests. \n\nWe still need to work through all the components of the ri.Insights (Cumulus) product and upgrade the log4J versions.   This epic will be collection of tasks that need to be implement to ensure we full upgrade log4J' issueType='Feature' status='Done' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,510 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48280 (CSTEST-463): summary='FAI Nav' description='FAI is an acronym for Femoral-Acetabular Impingement and refers to an arthroscopic sports medicine procedure in which the hip joint it re-shaped with a bur to improve hip flexion and reduce pain in the hip joint.  This specific ticket describes the development of an application which will be executed with the Cori platform to treat FAI through navigated resection of the femur neck.   At a high level the FAI Nav application will include the following components:\n\n* Software application which runs on the Cori console which includes UI & computation for tool calibration, guided image registration, and navigation of a cutting instrument. \n* Independent, mobile camera cart - During the FAI procedure the camera may need moved around the OR to maintain visibility with the tracked tools.  The cart mounted camera would make this difficult as the cart also houses the Cori console with handpiece, tablet, footpedal, tensioner cables running from it to the patient. \n* Collection Probe - Mechanical component used for characterization of the physical bone, necessary for image registration.  Analogous to the Cori Point Probe.  Will need to be tracked using the Cori tracking system.  \n* Cutting Tool - Mechanical component used for shaping the patients bone.  Will likely be an existing sports-med cutting wand with a tracking frame attached.  This tool will need to be tracked using the Cori tracking system.  \n\nNot included in this ticket is the development of an FAI Planning application which is necessary the deployment of an FAI but separate from the FAI Nav applicaiton.  The FAI Plan application will be run on a personal device (e.g. laptop or tablet), will use medical imaging and user interaction as input to create a resection plan to treat the patient specific case of FAI.  The plan that is the output of the ‘FAI Plan’ application will be the primary input for ‘FAI Nav’ application.' issueType='Epic' status='Review' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,510 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48524 (CSTEST-462): summary='Add case screenshots to case summary' description='Screenshots are captured during a CORI case which is part of the export folder for cases from all versions. Display the screenshots for each case as they are available.' issueType='Feature' status='Done' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,511 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48281 (CSTEST-461): summary='Kensci-Front End Features of Data Segmentation' description='' issueType='Epic' status="Won't Do" parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,512 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48525 (CSTEST-460): summary='Kensci-Global Deployments to Lumino Geographies' description='Cumulus V2 is tasked with deploying our current product into a number of OUS regions to support our existing CORI and RI.INSIGHTS footprint. Users from the specified regions will need access to their data and as a project team we must ensure that their data is located in regions that comply with local regulatory and data residency standards.\n\nThis ticket encompasses the work required from the Cumulus team to build and deploy the application and databases to the appropriate Lumino-supported geographies.' issueType='Feature' status='Done' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,512 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48526 (CSTEST-459): summary='ActiveArm: Disposable Blade for Saw End Effector' description='When using a https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-412 to complete a  https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-457 a sterile packed disposable blade will be required to make the bone cuts\n\nThe final COGS target for the saw blade (including sterilization and packaging) is $50.  This will make the COGS of the blade equivalent to the CORI bur.' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,513 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48527 (CSTEST-458): summary='Kensci-Backend Global Data Segmentation' description='This epic supports the KenSci Sync up to support ensuring the Cumulus application has ability to support globalized customers where users can be supported everywhere that KenSci currently supports.\n\nThis includes ensuring we have hosting and architecture that allows segmentation of the data into the following buckets:\n\n* Individual User - Cases that apply to the surgeon\n* Customer group of users - groups of users under the same customer\n* Country - Cases that originate from the same country\n* Regional - Cases that are in groups of countries (APAC, EMEA, LATAM, North America)\n\nCreate the backend and master authorizations for access to each level of data' issueType='Feature' status='Done' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,515 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48528 (CSTEST-457): summary='ActiveArm: TKA Saw Workflow' description='The active arm should be able to support a bone preparation workflow where a saw[https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-412|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-412|smart-link]  is automatically positioned[https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-393|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-393|smart-link]  to each femur and tibia cutting plane used in Smith + Nephew Total Knee Replacements and then used to execute each cut through the surgeon’s typical incision without the need for a cutting guide, pins or additional instrumentation\n\nThe cuts should be able to be completed in any order that the user prefers.  The users preference of cut order should be able to be saved and selected ahead of time. [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-465|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-465|smart-link] \n\nIn order to ensure a successful procedure it is important that the soft tissue structures of the knee are protected.  This can be accomplished through a combination of [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-396|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-396|smart-link] and [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-456|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-456|smart-link] \n\nThe time required to complete the cuts should be the same or less than completing a manual procedure.  The time requirement could be tested by completing a cadaveric time trial where a surgeon prepares 1 leg of the same specimen with the arm and 1 leg of the same specimen with manual instruments.\n\nThe following implants should be supported:\n\n* JOURNEY 2 CR and BCS\n* JOURNEY 2 CR Porous, JOURNEY 2 BCS Porous\n* LEGION CR and PS (Both Standard and Narrow)\n* LEGION CR Porous, LEGION PS Porous (Both Standard and Narrow)\n* GENESIS II CR and PS\n* ANTHEM CR and PS (Both Standard and Narrow)\n* Universal Cemented and CONCELOC baseplate\n\nConstraints:\n\n# Does not require additional instrumentation' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,515 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48529 (CSTEST-456): summary='ActiveArm Saw End Effector Tactile Feedback During Cutting' description='When using a saw end effector [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-412|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-412|smart-link] with the active arm both visibility and tactile feedback within the knee are limited compared to using a saw during a manual procedure. \n\nIn addition to [https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-396|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-396|smart-link] surgeons want to be able to have tactile feedback when the completing saw cuts for a total knee replacement\n\nUsability labs completed in 2021 utilized a joystick in order to control the robotic arm.  Feedback from these labs all expressed a concern that the surgeons felt too “disconnected” from the cutting tool.\n\nSpecific anatomical structures where additional tactile feedback is desired include include:\n\n* Transitions from cancellous to cortical bone \n* Popliteal Artery\n* Patellar Tendon\n* MCL\n* LCL\n* PCL\n* ACL' issueType='Feature' status='Funnel' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,516 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48282 (CSTEST-455): summary='Update CORI Operating System from Ubuntu 16' description='The current version of the CORI Ubuntu 16.04 operating system  is end of life.   We need to define the plan forward on how to upgrade the OS to a supported version.   \n\nThere is at least one customer that we have aggreged through a letter to ensure we are on a supported version of an Operating system by 2023.\n\n========================\n\n*Scope of Work*\n\n* Migrate CORI 2.0 baseline code to Ubuntu v22.0.4\n* Upgrade CORI Connectivity features (IRIS) baseline to Ubuntu 22.04\n* SOUP documentation of all tools/3rd party libraries which need upgrade to Ubuntu 22.04 to include following information\n** Name of the Software\n** The Software developer/provider\n** Version number\n** Build number, if available\n** Version release notes, if available\n** List of known bugs, if available\n* Dry Run Verification of CORI 2.1, post integration of Defect fixes, CORI Connectivity features and Ubuntu 22.04 upgrade on CORI 2.0' issueType='Epic' status='Done' parent=None
2026-07-20 22:41:52,516 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48283 (CSTEST-454): summary='Long Attachments Getting Stuck On Handpiece' description='An increasing number of complaints are being submitted (Qty. 16 in November(updated Nov 22) and 8 in October, see attachment),  where the user is unable to disconnect the long attachment from the handpiece after completing a CORI case.  When this happens the long attachment, handpiece tracker and handpiece must be replaced because they are unable to be separating in the field.  \n\nThis is likely due to a pin in the long attachment backing out in most cases.  \n\nThis ticket is requesting 2 actions:\n\n# Recommendation and supporting materials provided to the field that can mitigate this issue in the near term and reduce the pressure on handpiece inventory (see business case section).  This could be a recommendation to monitor the component and remove it from use prior to it getting stuck, limiting long attachment use to X number of uses, or similar instructions that would prevent the loss of a handpiece\n# Design change or manufacturing process improvement to prevent this failure mode from occurring\n\n\n\n[^report1637762267161.xls]\n\n \nSee below complaints for examples:\nCASE-2020-00011493\nCASE-2020-00013123\nCASE-2020-00014008\nCASE-2020-00015681\nCASE-2020-00017747\nCASE-2020-00017837\nCASE-2020-00018541\nCASE-2020-00018541\nCASE-2020-00018541\nCASE-2020-00018508\nCASE-2020-00018854\nCASE-2020-00021762\nCASE-2020-00033328\nCASE-2020-00034880\nCASE-2020-00035608\nCASE-2021-00037543\nCASE-2021-00041501\nCASE-2021-00042224\nCASE-2021-00042978\nCASE-2021-00044583\nCASE-2021-00044827\nCASE-2021-00047294\nCASE-2021-00047929\nCASE-2021-00049328\nCASE-2021-00049321\nCASE-2021-00050861\nCASE-2021-00052707\nCASE-2021-00052107\nCASE-2021-00053599\nCASE-2021-00057177\nCASE-2021-00062516\nCASE-2021-00065914\nCASE-2021-00068947\nCASE-2021-00069009\nCASE-2021-00072802\nCASE-2021-00074008\nCASE-2021-00074230\nCASE-2021-00075038\nCASE-20