2026-07-21 00:03:07,903 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48454 (CSTEST-672): summary='CORS Arbitrary Origin' description="h2. Threat Scenario\n\nThe RI.Insights application APIs use a cross-origin resource sharing (CORS) policy that allows access from any domain. This may allow a third party threat actor to retrieve sensitive data from a session or perform privileged actions on behalf of the user.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nAn Internet-based threat actor could target users of the RI.Insightsapplication by embedding script payloads or abusing Cross-Site Scripting vulnerabilities on other, often trusted, websites. When the payload is run in the browser of the target user, it could direct requests to the Cumulus application; if the target user is already authenticated to RI.Insights, the threat actor would be able to covertly issue and retrieve the results of the malicious requests as if they were issued legitimately by the authenticated user.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nAn anonymous Internet-based threat actor could discover this vulnerable condition by sending a request to RI.Insightswith a custom HTTP Origin header containing the domain of their choice. For instance, an attacker could create an HTTP Origin header containing 'Origin: [https://attack.l9group.com|https://attack.l9group.com/]' and then observe the response from the server that appends the chosen domain of the threat actor to the trusted Origin list.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\ncase-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nfaq-service-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nfile-transfer-service-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nhipdataprocess-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nlogger-service-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nlogger-service-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nnotification-service-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nprocedure-metadata-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\nusers-Release-2.1-master\\src\\main\\java\\org\\snn\\api\\ApiOriginFilter.java\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nCreate a CORS policy that only allows a trusted list of domains.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team initially discovered this issue after noting the wildcard Origin HTTP header explicitly set in the API filter source code. To validate the presence of the issue on the provided environment, comprehensive mapping of application assets and API endpoints was conducted. Each discovered asset or endpoint was then actively requested with a provided Origin header containing a wildcard or a domain specified and controlled by the assessment team. Each HTTP response was analyzed to determine how the server platform reacted to the Origin payload and in this case, affirming the source code discovery of the vulnerability, the application server responded with Access-Control-Allow-Origin header containing the wildcard value." issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:07,903 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48455 (CSTEST-671): summary='Docker container runs as USER root' description="h2. Threat Scenario\n\nMultiple runtime dependencies of the application are outdated and one bundled library contains high-risk vulnerabilities. Vulnerabilities that reside deep in dependencies or Third-Party Components (TPCs) typically survive in an application attack surface for extended periods of time. In contrast to vulnerabilities in custom application code, vulnerabilities in TPCs, once discovered publicly, are exploited in large horizontal operations by threat actors. Note that in this case, the affected component is currently listed as End-Of-Life (EOL) from the software vendor and security updates may not be available for this version.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nContainers that do not prevent applications from running as USER root could allow a threat actor who has gained access to the container to run code as a privileged (root) user. Applications running as root in the container that have been exploited could be used by the threat actor to execute commands / actions as the root user via the exploited application.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nThe assessment team examined the affected Docker configuration files and noted that commands are run as the USER root. The USER attribute is not set to a lower privilege user after the commands are run, resulting in the container running with root privileges. A threat actor that has compromised the containerized application could discern the process privilege attained.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\ns3-java-unzipper-eb-Release-2.1-master\\Dockerfile\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nAfter running the required privileged commands as the 'root' user, drop privileges by switching the user context to a normal unprivileged user for running the application.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team examined the affected Docker configuration files and noted that commands are run as the USER root. The USER attribute is not set to a lower privilege user after the commands are run, resulting in the container running with root privileges." issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:07,904 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48456 (CSTEST-670): summary='NoSQL Injection (DocumentDB)' description='h2. Threat Scenario\n\nA number of API parameters are vulnerable to backend query injection. The assessment team was able to reliably confirm the existence of these vulnerabilities by asking the database to sleep for a specific period of time after processing the query and then monitoring the response latency, but unable to leverage these vectors for a cybersecurity impact within the time of testing.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nUnknown at this time. The location of the queries and the configuration of the AWS ELB and WAF prevent commonly used methods of exploitation.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nAn authenticated attacker could invest in application mapping and parameter injection to discover the existence of these injection sources, but leveraging them would require significant investment in evasions.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\n[https://preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/case-service/case/component|https://preprod.real-intelligence.com/api/v1/case-service/case/component] (implant parameter)\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nLevel Nine recommends expanding the use of Spring DataBinder to strongly type incoming parameters, in the case of the implant parameter, constrain and validate the processed input to the list of Implant names supported by the application.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nThe assessment team issued a request to the case service with the "implant" parameter including payloads designed to delay the response. For example, the payload "%2b(select*from(select(sleep(15)))a)%2b" is designed to delay the response for 15 seconds and the response to this request took over 15 seconds to complete. This behavior indicates the payload was successfully evaluated server-side.' issueType='Task' status='Triage' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:07,904 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48457 (CSTEST-669): summary='Known Vulnerable Components' description="h2. Threat Scenario\n\nMultiple runtime dependencies of the application are outdated and one bundled library contains high-risk vulnerabilities. Vulnerabilities that reside deep in dependencies or Third-Party Components (TPCs) typically survive in an application attack surface for extended periods of time. In contrast to vulnerabilities in custom application code, vulnerabilities in TPCs, once discovered publicly, are exploited in large horizontal operations by threat actors. Note that in this case, the affected component is currently listed as End-Of-Life (EOL) from the software vendor and security updates may not be available for this version.\n\nh2. Potential Impact\n\nIf exploited, a vulnerability in a dependency may put the application at risk, resulting in compromise of the entire application. The assessment team was not able to validate an exploitable path to the dependency vulnerabilities.\n\nh2. Threat Discovery\n\nDiscovery of this issue is limited to threat actors with access to the application binaries, source code repositories, or installer / update packages. The assessment team observed this issue during generation and review of the Software Composition Analysis (SCA) artifacts.\n\nh2. Affected Components\n\nfrontend-Release-2.1-master:\nejs <3.1.7 CVE-2022-29078\nloader-utils 2.0.0 - 2.0.3 CVE-2022-37601\nshell-quote <=1.7.2 CVE-2021-42740\n\ninvoke-cori-data-processor-lambda-sam-Release-2.1-master:\nasync 3.2.0 CVE-2021-43138\n\nMultiple repositories:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson 2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\norg.json:json 20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\nh2. Remediation Recommendation\n\nLevel Nine recommends that all vulnerable dependencies listed should be updated to their latest versions. In many cases, this will require major semantic version upgrades. On-going monitoring of development and runtime dependencies for security updates, as well as integrating and testing those updates should be a security priority.\n\nh2. Technical Details\n\nLevel Nine performed indexing and cross-referencing of dependencies derived from the source code repositories provided by Smith and Nephew. In this process, declared dependencies are typically found in a language or ecosystem specific project file in each repository, for example 'package.json' in NodeJS applications. This list of library names and versions required by the application is cross-referenced with open-source vulnerability intelligence sources, such as the National Vulnerability Database (NVD) hosted by NIST. A full list of vulnerabilities associated with each Third-Party Component (TPC) is provided below, with a summary of the highest-risk findings provided in the Affected Components section of this report.\n\ncase-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson v2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\ncommons-io:commons-io v2.6 CVE-2021-29425\norg.json:json v20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\nfaq-service-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson v2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\norg.json:json v20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\nfile-transfer-service-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson v2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\ncommons-io:commons-io v2.6 CVE-2021-29425\norg.json:json v20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\nhipdataprocess-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson v2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\ncommons-io:commons-io 2.6 vCVE-2021-29425\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j v1.2.5 CVE-2022-25647\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j v1.2.5 CVE-2018-1002202\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j v1.2.5 CVE-2022-24615\norg.json:json v20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\ninvoke-cori-data-processor-lambda-sam-Release-2.1-master:\nasyn v3.2.0 CVE-2021-43138\n\ninvoke-dashboard-aggregation-trigger-Release-2.1-master:\njunit:junit 4.12 CVE-2020-15250\norg.json:json 20170516 CVE-2022-45688\n\ninvoke-hipdata-processor-lambda-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson 2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\njunit:junit 4.12 CVE-2020-15250\norg.json:json 20170516 CVE-2022-45688\ncom.google.code.gson:gson 2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\njunit:junit 4.12 CVE-2020-15250\norg.json:json 20170516 CVE-2022-45688\n\nnotification-service-Release-2.1-master:\ncom.google.code.gson:gson 2.8.6 CVE-2022-25647\ncommons-io:commons-io 2.6 CVE-2021-29425\norg.json:json 20200518 CVE-2022-45688\n\ns3-java-unzipper-eb-Release-2.1-master:\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j 1.2.5 CVE-2022-25647\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j 1.2.5 CVE-2018-1002202\nnet.lingala.zip4j:zip4j 1.2.5 CVE-2022-24615\norg.apache.httpcomponents:httpclient 4.5.9 CVE-2020-13956\norg.json:json 20220924 CVE-2022-45688\n\nfrontend-Release-2.1-master:\nansi-html <0.0.8 CVE-2021-23424\nejs <3.1.7 CVE-2022-29078\nfast-xml-parser <4.2.4 CVE-2023-34104\nglob-parent <5.1.2 CVE-2020-28469\nimmer <9.0.6 CVE-2021-3757\nloader-utils 2.0.0 - 2.0.3 CVE-2022-37601\nminimatch <3.0.5 CVE-2022-3517\nnth-check <2.0.1 CVE-2021-3803\nshell-quote <=1.7.2 CVE-2021-42740\n\ncrp-converter-master/CrpEdit/CrpEdit.exe:\nlibjpeg 6b CVE-2020-14152\nzlib 1.2.11 CVE-2022-37434\n\ncrp-converter-master/CrpEdit/nddscore.dll:\nzlib 1.2.11 CVE-2022-37434\n\ncrp-converter-master/CrpEdit/libssl-1_1-x64.dll:\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2021-3711\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2021-3712\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-0778\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-1292\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-2068\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-4450\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0215\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0286\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0464\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-2650\n\ncrp-converter-master/CrpEdit/libcrypto-1_1-x64.dll:\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2021-3711\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2021-3712\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-0778\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-1292\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-2068\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2022-4450\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0215\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0286\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-0464\nopenssl 1.1.1k CVE-2023-2650\n\ncrp-converter-master/CrpEdit/nddssecurity.dll:\nopenssl 1.0.2 CVE-2015-1789 HIGH\nopenssl 1.0.2 CVE-2015-3193 HIGH\nopenssl 1.0.2 CVE-2015-3194 HIGH\nopenssl 1.0.2 CVE-2016-0705 CRITICAL\nopenssl 1.0.2 C