2026-07-21 00:03:08,183 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48391 (CSTEST-142): summary='Joint Line Tibia' description='+*FEATURE DESCRIPTION*+\nThis feature is to use CORI to define the landmarks, measurements, and project the native tibial joint line during the registration process while the old implant is still attached. This feature includes the necessary stories to define the tibia joint line and support increasing the joint line distance from any landmarks to a point the surgeon deems appropriate.\n\nThe joint line will be calculated by CORI and rendered in CORI\'s UI on the bone mesh so that the surgeon can make a judgement if the line is in the appropriate place. The landmark point could be put in any location, the surgeon choose on the anatomy and the joint line should be calculated some distance away. This distance could be based upon a research papers that say 25mm on average is a good value or based upon the surgeon setting the value in OR from anatomical considerations. This requires some widget to be able to move the joint line up and down from CORI\'s measures or predicted location.\n\n+*CONTEXT*+\nThe identification of the "native" or original joint line with the implant still on the patient is key to planning where the new implant should be placed. There are several methods for establishing the native joint line, one is to identify the tibia joint line based upon landmarks during the registration process.\n\n+*CONTEXT*+\nThis allows the user to place a landmark point at the fibial head (point where the fibia attaches to the tibia) then measure up a certain distance to measure where the "native" joint line should be.\n\nThis could be done two ways:\nOne where we measure from an x-ray where we measure and then apply that distance in CORI through the Joint Line Toggle UI\n\nAnother method would be to use statistics and apply that distance to the landmark to locate the joint.\n\n+*BENEFIT HYPOTHESIS*+\nRestoring the native tibia joint line during the RKA procedure is another figure of merit used by surgeon to measure if the revision procedure was successful, having this feature is thereby necessary as a significant number of surgeons will expect to have this feature.\n\n+*ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA*+\n\n\n# Tibia native jointline is rendered on the tibia bone mesh\n# Surgeons can toggle the tibia joint line up or down to their desired distance\n# This reference jointline is available to be used in implant planning\n# \n\n\n+*ARCHITECTURAL RUNWAY NEEDS*+\n\n# Need to capture the new landmarks\n# Need to show the show the surgeon guidance on how to collect landmarks\n# Calculator to create and render jointline on bone mesh\n# Toggle widget to move the line up and down\n# Jointline shown in Planning\n# \n\n\n+*REFERENCES*+\n\n# joint_line.pdf\n# Feature Story Map - [https://prolaborate.sncloudcontrol.com/LG2NM9|https://prolaborate.sncloudcontrol.com/LG2NM9|smart-link] \n\n\n+*OPEN QUESTIONS*+' issueType='Epic' status='Archive' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:08,184 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48392 (CSTEST-141): summary='Tibia Bone Refinement in Registration' description='+*FEATURE DESCRIPTION*+\nThis feature describes a need to create a surface which approximates the post explant tibia bone surface. The post-explant bone surface needs to be referenced during planning and also needs to be used to create a volumentric cutting model for bone removal.\n\n+*CONTEXT*+\nThis feature is needed for addition of RKA functionality. Upon completion of point collection in free collection, the software needs to create a volumetric model that can be refined to depict the surface of the bone once the original implant is removed. This will be used during planning to visualize.\nThis has a dependency on the hardware that will be used to refine: using the CORI handpiece to refine or using the CORI pointer probe with a new refining guard.\n\nSimilar to the bone refinement stage in F/T bone removal, however, we need to have this stage prior to final planning.\n\n+*INCLUDES*+\n\n\n# Creation of a volumetric model upon collection of sufficient free collection points.\n# Potentially select tool used for refining or determine based on proximity to the bone.\n# Allow for refining using handpiece\n# Allow also for refining using the pointer probe \\[Need to connect with the HW team to get the dimensions]\n# \n\n\n+*BENEFIT HYPOTHESIS*+\n\nProvides ability to characterize the bone without the primary implant on the surface.\n\nTo be used to visualize and plan for placement of RKA imlpant and Augments\n\n+*ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA*+\nThe surgeon is able to create a surface representation of the post-explant bone surface. That surface is visible in planning. That surface can be made into a volumetric cutting model to be burred in bone removal.\n\n\n\n+*ARCHITECTURAL RUNWAY NEEDS*+\n\n* Add new surface/ cut model to datastore\n\n\n\n+*REFERENCES*+\n\n# See current bone refinement stage in bone removal\n# Prolaborate Link:\xa0[https://prolaborate.sncloudcon...|https://prolaborate.sncloudcontrol.com/7pjccl]\n# \n\n\n+*OPEN QUESTIONS*+\n\xa0\n\n# Should it be an option / mode from Femur free collection? Activated after atleast 25 points are collected in free collection. Benefits of doing this: It can always be an option in TKA.\n# Can this be a push/pop navigation from planning? Benefits: reduces the number of times that the surgeon needs to take the primary implant on and off of the bone while keeping the TKA gap assessment stage where it is in the workflow. Risks: initial planning stage has no information about post-explant bone surface which is the primary surface referenced in RKA Planning.\n# Should it be between gap assessment and planning with gaps?\n# Or should it be a new stage right before planning similar to ER0665\n#' issueType='Epic' status='Archive' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:08,184 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48393 (CSTEST-140): summary='Bone Removal - Bur all workpiece for Revision' description='+*FEATURE DESCRIPTION*+\nThis feature displays the bone regions to cut and allows user to cut bone\n\n+*CONTEXT*+\nThis is the refined model\nthis includes augments\n\n\n\n+*BENEFIT HYPOTHESIS*+\nThis state provides different visual aids that reflect the outcomes of the user’s actions. It also provides several controls to aid the cutting process.\n\n+*ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA*+\n\n\n# Stage appears upon completion of planning\n# User is presented with a visualization of the workpiece\n# Pockets to be cut and depths are represented in colour\n# User selectable rotation options\n# Different visualization modes\n# Display of HP and workpiece as bone is cut\n# Options to change bur and pan from this screen\n# \n\n\n+*ARCHITECTURAL RUNWAY NEEDS*+\n\n\n\n+*REFERENCES*+\n\n# DD0085\n# Prolaborate Link:\xa0[https://prolaborate.sncloudcon...|https://prolaborate.sncloudcontrol.com/ISwY9]\n# Jira Link:\xa0[https://smith-nephew.atlassian...|https://smith-nephew.atlassian.net/browse/CORI-140]\u200b\n# \n\n\n+*OPEN QUESTIONS*+' issueType='Epic' status='Archive' parent=None
2026-07-21 00:03:08,185 - INFO - Fetched source issue 48394 (CSTEST-139): summary='Create Revision compatible IDB configuration' description='+*FEATURE DESCRIPTION*+\nThis is the Legion Revision Knee (RK) implant addition to the IDB. The Legion RK has challenges associated with its introduction to the IDB, because it uses screw on stems and augments (aka wedges) that can be added to the implant in multiple configurations that CORI must keep track of.\n\nLegion RK implant will not be available to anyone who has not purchased Legion RK.\n\n+*CONTEXT*+\nThe RK implant is different than the CR and PS, it has a 6 degree valgus offset stem.\nThe Legion RK will be a new implant type.\n\nLegion PS - in TKA\nLegion PS + augmented - only available with RKA\nLegion RK - only available with RKA\n\nNeed to figure out what is in scope for the IDB. The IDB structure needs to be generically defined to support augments such that the same augment can support two different primary implants.\n\nOne option could be to specify which implant sizes are compatible with the specific augments through part numbers.\n\nCheck the default slope values match the surgical technique handbook.\n\n+*BENEFIT HYPOTHESIS*+\nThe Legion RK is the primary implant that will be used in Revision it is therefor necessary to be included in the RKA scope. Also the Legion implant costs 3x more than the regular TKA implant. It is therefore business necessary to get Legion out there.\n\n+*ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA*+\n\n\n# Legion RK Can be selected in RKA\n# Legion RK can not be selected in TKA\n# Q40 has been followed\n# \n\n\n+*ARCHITECTURAL RUNWAY NEEDS*+\n\n\n\n+*REFERENCES*+\n\n# Prolaborate Link:\xa0[https://prolaborate.sncloudcon...|https://prolaborate.sncloudcontrol.com/cNRG4]\n# \n\n\n+*OPEN QUESTIONS*+\n\n# Will we consider this implant as useable for TKA as well as RKA?\n# Will a surgeon implant an RK implant without performing an RKA? Will Legion RK be a primary implant in TKA?\n#' issueType='Epic' status='Archive' parent=None
